WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.510
So, in this video, I just want to say a

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.630
little bit on the use of generative AI

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.830
while you're studying, particularly

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.870
while you're studying for this course on

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.510
an introduction to digital design using

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.190
VHDL.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.350
So, clearly AI is out there. It's widely

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.030
used. The marketing says generative AI

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.990
assists in coding by automating

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.070
repetitive tasks, generating code

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.590
snippets from natural language prompts,

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.470
debugging, refactoring, and improving

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.950
code quality and security. Certainly, it

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.150
can do all of those things.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.790
The reality, and I'm going to use quotes

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.870
from Linus Torvalds, or as he calls it

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.590
autocorrect on steroids, I think that's

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.750
a good description, too.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.910
Often wrong, always entertaining. So,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.430
the problem is

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.310
it doesn't always give the right answer.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.710
And you as an engineer are fully

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.790
responsible for the design.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.790
So, you need enough knowledge to know if

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.830
what the AI is producing is good, is

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.310
first of all correct, and also whether

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.190
it's actually a good design.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.350
So, in order to use generative AI in

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.990
your coding, or in this case in hardware

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.430
design, you need to know

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.910
what is correct or good.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.990
And you'll only learn that by practicing

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.430
and doing it by yourself.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.110
Okay?

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.910
So, and and the other thing to bear in

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.630
mind with the use is it is cheaper to

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.390
get it right without AI,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.150
write yourself, than to use use AI to

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.150
generate loads of code and then go back

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.990
and have to fix it later. So, it's

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.190
always easier to correct problems, to

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.550
debug, as close as possible to the

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.030
source of the problem. That's that's

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.590
just a general principle, and certainly

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.150
my experience.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.830
So, it's much better to get it right,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.350
without, you know, as as soon and test

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.190
it

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.670
as close as possible to the unit that

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.230
you're that that you're interested in.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.190
So, just to say a few uses of generative

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.270
AI in assignments in general, this is

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.990
more of those of you who might be doing

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.990
assignments for the university. Uh the

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.790
first thing I should say is GitHub

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.469
Copilot AI is embedded in VS Code, which

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:04.870
we're using in this.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.350
So, be The first thing I'm going to say

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.990
if you're doing this for if you're doing

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.870
your any design work for any university,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.870
be aware and follow the particular

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.310
university's policies and rules. They'll

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.310
certainly have some. If they say you're

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.870
not allowed to use it, then don't use

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.390
it.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.470
Um

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.150
that if you are going to use it in your

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.949
design, you must at the end you must be

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:25.590
able to answer questions about your

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.070
submitted work. And if there's an oral

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.590
examination, they might give you an oral

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:29.990
examination. I certainly use oral

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.990
examinations. If you can't answer a

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.590
question in your own work, that's

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.350
probably going to be a problem and

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.390
affect your mark.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.750
Um if you do use an AI tool to help

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.830
generate code, um you must indicate this

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.150
in in any submitted work and explain how

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.750
you've modified or adapted the code

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.550
to make it your own work. So, what

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.910
changes you've made.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.070
And just to say again in a university

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.870
context or in a assessed context,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.510
failure to do so would be considered

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.910
academic misconduct and could have quite

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.470
dire consequences.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.070
So, in terms of GitHub Copilot

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.910
integration, I am going to suggest that

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.710
we can use AI, but not to do the design

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.030
for you.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.190
So, what I have done is I have given you

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.550
a prompt. I have embedded in the set of

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.390
exercises that you download a

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.270
description of good practice in terms of

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.550
the design of

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.190
VHDL. So, that's in the repository that

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.870
you download to use.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.030
I've also provided a code reviewer

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.430
chat mode or agent I think they call

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:31.790
them agents now

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.870
provided and you and you can use that to

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.949
provide feedback. And that's the key

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:38.590
thing here. The idea is, and this is my

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.110
recommended workflow when you're doing

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.750
the exercises,

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.350
turn off auto complete or auto correct.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.750
The reason is that that will give you

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.270
suggested things and actually they will

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.710
distract you and slow you down.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.190
Because I'm experienced, they actually

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.950
can be quite useful and save me typing

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.350
because I know if they're right or wrong

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.470
and I can immediately, you know, either

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.190
accept it or not. For you For if you

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.430
start out, you you will find it that it

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.950
will slow you down.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:04.870
So,

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.110
turn off auto correct. Don't use it

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.470
while you're learning.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.190
Um create your own working solution uh

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.949
based on on, you know, the information

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:16.509
I've given you without AI at all. So,

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:17.750
we'll turn off, as I said, turn off the

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.110
auto correct.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.190
I've provided test benches or in one

